// StdLoweringShared — the TARGET-INDEPENDENT half of the Std → Target lowering, shared by BOTH
// `StdToX64Conversion` and `StdToArm64Conversion` so the one fact each answers has ONE home.
//
// Two things live here, and both are target-independent by construction — they read only a Std TYPE,
// never an ISA:
//   • the memory-access DOORS (`accessWidthFor`, `memoryDisplacement`) — a Std type / byte offset
//     mapped to the neutral `X64AccessWidth` and the `Imm32` displacement the shared `loadRegBaseDisp`
//     / `storeBaseDispReg` ops carry (the ENCODER that consumes them is per-ISA; the width is not).
//   • the VALUE-CLASS COLUMN producer (`buildValueClassColumns`) — the register FILE each SSA value
//     lands in. It is produced HERE because this is the last pass that can see a Std type: the Target
//     tier carries none (see `ValueClassColumn` in TargetDialect), and the allocator downstream
//     colours within a class it can no longer derive.
//
// ⭐ WHY SHARED AND NOT FORKED: a value's class is what its type says, IDENTICALLY for x64 and arm64.
// When this walk was forked, the two copies had already drifted — the x64 copy skips the target-only
// blocks the float-equality split mints, the arm64 copy did not — and the day a new `StdOp` learned to
// define a value, one target would have silently misclassified it (a float into a GPR) while the other
// stayed correct. One walk, checked once by the compiler's exhaustiveness, cannot drift.

// ⭐ **WHICH STANDARD STREAM — the ONLY thing `osWriteStdout` and `osWriteStderr` differ by (W35).**
// The two ops carry the same operands, the same metadata and the same result; every backend therefore
// lowers ONE graph and reads its own ABI's spelling of the stream off this — a `GetStdHandle` selector on
// Windows, a POSIX descriptor on Linux and macOS, a `wasi:cli` accessor import on wasm. Stating it as one
// enum is what keeps a backend from serving one stream and forgetting the other: the map from this to that
// backend's constant is exhaustive, so the day a THIRD stream exists every backend fails to compile.
//
// ⚠ It lives HERE and not in a target file for `buildValueClassColumns`'s reason — it is target-independent
// by construction (it names a stream, never an ISA) and it has three readers, so a per-backend copy is the
// fork that file's header warns about. It is deliberately NOT the panic writer's stderr:
// `BacktraceFormat.WriteStderrName` is a hand-assembled cstring chunk, a different mechanism (see
// `StdOp.osWriteStderr`).
export enum OsOutputStream
	standardOutput
	standardError
end 'OsOutputStream'

// ⭐ **THE TWO POSIX STANDARD DESCRIPTORS, IN ONE PLACE — which is the whole point of the pair (W35
// review).** Each is a POSIX fact rather than a per-OS one: x64-linux's `write` syscall, arm64-linux's
// `svc` and arm64-macOS's libSystem `_write` all name the same two numbers. Windows has no descriptor
// for either — its writers ask `GetStdHandle` for a selector instead (`X64Runtime.StdErrorHandle`,
// `StdToX64Conversion.StdOutputHandle`), which is why that family is spelled in the x64 files.
//
// ⚠ **THEY WERE IN TWO FILES, AND EACH OF THEM ALSO HAD A SECOND SPELLING SOMEWHERE ELSE.** `StderrFd`
// lived in `BacktraceFormat.maxon` because the hand-assembled panic writer was once its only reader;
// stdout was `X64LinuxRuntime.WriteStdoutFd`, read by the x64 isel alone. W35 gave the Std tier a
// `printError` and a shared `posixStreamFd`, which made each of those a fact with two homes — so the
// descriptors were brought together here and the x64-Linux copy was deleted. `printError`'s descriptor
// and the panic writer's are the same number and must stay one declaration; that they belong to two
// unrelated MECHANISMS (see `StdOp.osWriteStderr`) is a fact about the code that reads them, not about
// the number.
export let PosixStdoutFd = 0x1  // fd = 1 (STDOUT_FILENO)
export let StderrFd = 0x2  // fd = 2 (STDERR_FILENO) — also the panic/backtrace writer's destination

// ⭐ THE ONE MAP from a standard stream to the POSIX descriptor a `write` names — asked by BOTH native
// POSIX lowerings (x64-linux's raw syscall and arm64's syscall/libSystem call), so the two isels cannot come
// to disagree about which descriptor `printError` writes to. Exhaustive over `OsOutputStream`, which is what
// makes a third stream a compile error at every backend rather than a silent default.
export function posixStreamFd(stream OsOutputStream) returns ParsedInt
	return match stream 's'
		standardOutput gives PosixStdoutFd
		standardError gives StderrFd
	end 's'
end 'posixStreamFd'

// ⭐ THE ONE DOOR from a Std type to a memory-access WIDTH. `i1`/`u8` are one byte, `i64`/`f64` the
// full word; those are what the language can put behind a load/store today. Every other type panics
// rather than picking a nearby width — a narrow integer needs the `as` cast (P1.9), and its SIGNEDNESS
// then decides the sign/zero-extending load form. Reuses the neutral `X64AccessWidth` enum: the width
// (HOW MANY BITS) is not ISA-specific; only the encoder that turns `byte`/`word64` into the concrete
// instruction (`movzx`/`mov` vs `ldrb`/`ldr`) is. An `f64` access is 8 bytes; whether it moves through
// the integer or FP form is decided at emit time by the register's class, in each backend.
export function accessWidthFor(t StdType) returns X64AccessWidth
	match t 'w'
		i1 then return X64AccessWidth.byte
		i64 then return X64AccessWidth.word64
		f64 then return X64AccessWidth.word64
		i8 to i32 then panic("accessWidthFor: no memory-access form for {t.name} — a narrow integer needs the `as` cast (P1.9), and its SIGNEDNESS then decides the sign/zero-extending load rather than defaulting to a width")
		// A `u8` access is unambiguous BECAUSE it is unsigned: zero-extension is always correct, so no
		// signed/unsigned choice is deferred. Wider unsigned types stay deferred.
		u8 then return X64AccessWidth.byte
		u16 to u64 then panic("accessWidthFor: no memory-access form for {t.name} — an unsigned type wider than a byte needs the `as` cast (P1.9)")
		f32 then panic("accessWidthFor: f32 is 4 bytes and moves through a single-precision form, a width this enum does not carry; Maxon's `float` is f64, so nothing produces one")
	end 'w'
end 'accessWidthFor'

// A memory-band byte offset as the signed `Imm32` displacement a `[base + disp]` load/store carries.
// `ByteOffset` is unsigned and unbounded; the `Imm32` field is signed 32-bit, so the narrowing is
// CHECKED rather than cast away. This is the field's own encodable bound; each backend's ENCODER
// enforces the tighter per-ISA reach (x64 disp32, arm64's scaled/unscaled forms) on top of it. Every
// offset today is a global-slot offset (0); the bound starts mattering at P1.1's struct-field offsets.
export function memoryDisplacement(offset ByteOffset) returns Imm32
	if offset > i32.max 'tooFar'
		panic("memoryDisplacement: byte offset {offset} does not fit the signed Imm32 displacement of a base+disp load/store — an addressing mode reaching that far from its base needs a materialized index register")
	end 'tooFar'
	return offset as Imm32
end 'memoryDisplacement'

// Callee name -> its `ArgFloatMask`. Built once per module so a call site's per-argument register-FILE
// decision is an O(1) lookup rather than a Θ(calls × functions) scan of `stdModule.functions` for the
// callee's parameter types.
//
// A MASK, not the `Map with (ByteArray, RegClassArray)` an array-shaped mirror would be: a parameter's
// class is ONE bit (GPR vs XMM), and a per-callee array would heap-box one small array for EVERY function
// in the module — most all-integer and never the target of a float-argument call. The scalar mask keeps
// this index allocation-flat (a scalar per entry). The bit is read back as a `RegClass` by
// `calleeParamClass`, the one door the caller-side argument routing consults.
//
// ⚠ **ITS RESULT-SIDE TWIN NO LONGER EXISTS (A2l).** `CalleeResultClassMap` held
// `returnTypeResultClass(func.returnType)` — a match over a field, not a fold — so it was a SECOND COPY of
// something `FunctionNameIndex` already reaches in two O(1) steps, keyed on a uniqueness guarantee it did
// not state. `calleeResultClass` reads through the index instead; there is one fewer name-keyed map per
// native compile, and one fewer place the key set could disagree with the module's.
export typealias CalleeParamClassMap = Map with (ByteArray, ArgFloatMask)

// ⭐ THE ONE DOOR from a Std type to a register FILE. A value's class is what its TYPE says, and
// nothing else — the whole content of `ValueClassColumn`'s "produced by the lowering, not derived in
// the backend".
function regClassForStdType(t StdType) returns RegClass
	return RegClass.xmm if stdTypeIsFloat(t) else RegClass.gpr
end 'regClassForStdType'

// The class of a UNARY op's result, which is NOT its operand's whenever the op CROSSES files.
//
// ⚠⚠ THE CONVERSIONS INVERT. `siToFp`'s operand is an INT and its result is an f64; `fpToSi`'s operand
// is an f64 and its result is an int. `operandType` is the SOURCE by definition, so the conversion band
// — and `bitcastF64ToI64`, which crosses the same boundary without converting — are the ops whose
// result type is not on the op at all. `neg`/`bitNot`/`logicalNot` stay in their operand's file: they
// compute what they consume.
function unaryResultClass(opcode StdUnaryOpcode, operandType StdType) returns RegClass
	return match opcode 'k'
		neg to logicalNot gives regClassForStdType(operandType)
		// int -> f64: the result lives in an XMM/SIMD register though `operandType` is an integer.
		siToFp gives RegClass.xmm
		// f64 -> int: the result lives in a GPR though `operandType` is f64.
		fpToSi gives RegClass.gpr
		// ⚠ The MATH INTRINSICS need their OWN arm even though their result class IS their operand's,
		// and that is the whole trap of appending past `fpToSi`: `regClassForStdType(operandType)` would
		// be the right answer, but the arm that computes it stops at `logicalNot` and cannot be extended
		// to reach here without swallowing both conversions on the way. Stating `xmm` outright is also
		// the stronger claim — these are f64 → f64 by definition (`stdUnaryOperandIsFloat`), so the
		// class does not depend on `operandType` at all.
		floor to abs gives RegClass.xmm
		// ⚠ The BITCAST is the THIRD shape this table has to answer for, and the only one whose
		// operand and result differ in FILE without differing in BITS: `bitcastF64ToI64` moves an
		// f64's pattern into a GPR unchanged. `operandType` is the f64 SOURCE, so — exactly as for
		// `fpToSi` — reading it as the result would put a 64-bit integer in an XMM register.
		bitcastF64ToI64 gives RegClass.gpr
		// The INBOUND bitcast is the same claim mirrored, and it is the reason the arm above may not
		// simply become a range: `operandType` here is the i64 SOURCE, so reading it would put an f64
		// in a GPR — the exact inverse mistake, from the exact same misreading.
		bitcastI64ToF64 gives RegClass.xmm
	end 'k'
end 'unaryResultClass'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH ROUNDING `floor`/`ceil`/`round` ASK FOR** — the ONE place that decision is made, for the
// same reason `accessWidthFor` and `unaryResultClass` live in this file: it reads a Std OPCODE and
// nothing else, so it is target-independent by construction, and BOTH native isels need it.
//
// ⚠⚠ IT WAS FORKED, AND THE FORK WAS THE DANGEROUS KIND. `StdToX64Conversion` and
// `StdToArm64Conversion` each spelled the same three `opcode → FloatRoundMode` pairs into their own
// `match`, one ISA apart, with nothing making the copies agree. `FloatRoundMode` being a shared enum
// does NOT make the MAPPING shared — it only makes both copies spell the same member names. Change
// `ceil` to `towardNegative` in one isel and neither site reads as wrong: x64 would quietly compute
// `floor` where the program said `ceil` while arm64 stayed correct, and the answer — not the build —
// is what breaks. Worse asymmetrically: the x64 half is pinned by committed goldens
// (`roundsdRegReg.towardPositive`), the arm64 half only by goldens no Windows or Linux host can
// regenerate, so the copy with the weaker gate is the one free to drift.
//
// `sqrt`, `abs` and BOTH bitcasts are deliberately absent, for two different reasons: neither
// `sqrt` nor `abs` is a `roundsd`/`frint` at all (each has its own lowering on each ISA), and a bitcast
// computes nothing to round. Asking this function for any of them is a lowering bug, not a missing arm.
export function floatRoundModeFor(opcode StdUnaryOpcode) returns FloatRoundMode
	match opcode 'k'
		floor then return FloatRoundMode.towardNegative
		ceil then return FloatRoundMode.towardPositive
		round then return FloatRoundMode.nearestTiesToEven
		neg to fpToSi then panic("floatRoundModeFor: Std unary opcode {opcode.name} performs no rounding — only floor/ceil/round lower to a rounding instruction (`roundsd` on x64, `frint` on arm64), and the isels route only those three here")
		// ONE arm for the two math intrinsics that are not roundings: the reason is identical (each has
		// its own lowering, which is not a `roundsd`/`frint` mode) and only the instructions differ, so
		// the message names both rather than the same sentence being written twice.
		sqrt to abs then panic("floatRoundModeFor: `{opcode.name}` has no rounding mode — `sqrt` is its own instruction on both ISAs (`sqrtsd` / `fsqrt`) and `abs` clears the sign bit (a `psllq`/`psrlq` pair on x64, `fabs` on arm64); an isel asking either for its rounding has routed it to the wrong op")
		// ONE arm for BOTH bitcasts, and this is the one table where spanning them is right: a
		// reinterpretation computes no number IN EITHER DIRECTION, so the answer does not depend on
		// which file the bits are moving to. The arms that DO depend on the direction — the operand
		// domain and both result-class tables — name them separately, as `StdUnaryOpcode`'s header says.
		bitcastF64ToI64 to bitcastI64ToF64 then panic("floatRoundModeFor: `{opcode.name}` REINTERPRETS the operand's bits and computes no number at all, so there is nothing for it to round — an isel asking for its rounding has routed it to the wrong op")
	end 'k'
end 'floatRoundModeFor'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH END OF THE ORDERING `min`/`max` KEEP** — the exact twin of `floatRoundModeFor` above,
// living here for the same reason: it reads a Std OPCODE and nothing else, so it is target-independent
// by construction, and ALL THREE isels need it.
//
// It exists so the `min`/`max` distinction is decided ONCE rather than in each backend's `match`. That
// is not a hypothetical: the rounding mapping WAS forked across two isels and had to be extracted, and
// this one would have forked across three — x64 choosing an opcode byte, arm64 a condition code, wasm a
// comparison instruction. All three would still compile with the wrong one, and every ORDERED test
// would still pass, because `min` and `max` differ only on ordered-and-distinct operands, which is
// exactly what a per-backend copy would keep getting right by accident on some targets and not others.
//
// `div` is the third float-only opcode and is deliberately absent: it is neither a `min` nor a `max`,
// it lowers to one native instruction on every target, and it never reaches a compare-and-select.
// Asking this for any non-`min`/`max` opcode is a lowering bug, not a missing arm.
export function floatMinMaxKindFor(opcode StdBinOpcode) returns FloatMinMaxKind
	match opcode 'k'
		min then return FloatMinMaxKind.min
		max then return FloatMinMaxKind.max
		add to div then panic("floatMinMaxKindFor: `{opcode.name}` is not a compare-and-select — only `min` and `max` pick an end of the ordering, and the isels route only those two here")
	end 'k'
end 'floatMinMaxKindFor'

// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPARISON A `min`/`max` SELECT IS BUILT ON — AND IT IS THE LANGUAGE'S OWN FLOAT `<`
// AND `>`, NOT A SECOND SET OF CONDITIONS INVENTED FOR THE INTRINSICS.** `min(a, b)` is `a` when
// `a < b` and `max(a, b)` is `a` when `a > b`, so this returns exactly the predicate that sentence
// names, and the two targets that BUILD a select (arm64, wasm) each feed it to the float-compare
// mapping they already own — `arm64FloatCondForPred` and `emitFloatCmpPred`.
//
// ⭐ That is what makes the NaN behaviour a CONSEQUENCE rather than a coincidence. Both of those
// mappings are already NaN-safe, because IEEE-754 requires every relational predicate to be false on
// unordered and a source-level `a < b` must obey that. Routing through them means `min`/`max` cannot
// drift from `<`/`>` on a NaN without a source comparison drifting too — and there are spec cases on
// float comparison. A backend that instead picked its own condition here would be re-deciding the
// unordered rule, per ISA, with nothing holding the copies together; that is the exact shape of the
// fork `floatRoundModeFor` above was extracted to close.
//
// x64 does not call this: `minsd`/`maxsd` ARE the compare-and-select, so its isel needs the
// `FloatMinMaxKind` and an opcode byte, not a predicate.
export function floatMinMaxSelectPred(kind FloatMinMaxKind) returns StdCmpPred
	return match kind 'k'
		min gives StdCmpPred.lt
		max gives StdCmpPred.gt
	end 'k'
end 'floatMinMaxSelectPred'

// The class a function's RETURN lands in. `void` is `gpr` because a void call still defines a value —
// see `calleeResultClass`.
function returnTypeResultClass(returnType StdReturnType) returns RegClass
	return match returnType 'r'
		void gives RegClass.gpr
		value(inner) gives regClassForStdType(inner)
	end 'r'
end 'returnTypeResultClass'

// Write `valueId`'s class into the column, EXTENDING it when the id runs past the operand-count
// pre-size. The pre-size (`scanFunctionValueCount`) counts OPERAND-used values; this walk records a
// class for every value an op DEFINES, and those are not the same set (a fused loop-test `cmp` defines
// a boolean that lives only in flags and is never an operand). `growFilled` is amortized-linear and
// the added slots are `gpr`, the same fill the pre-size uses for ids no op names.
function setValueClass(column ValueClassColumn, valueId ValueId, class RegClass)
	if valueId >= column.count() 'extend'
		column.growFilled(valueId + 1, value: RegClass.gpr)
	end 'extend'

	try column.set(valueId, value: class) otherwise panic("setValueClass: v{valueId} outside the {column.count()}-wide class column after growFilled(v{valueId}+1) — a grow that just ensured the slot cannot leave it missing")
end 'setValueClass'

// ⭐⭐ THE VALUE-CLASS COLUMN PRODUCER. One dense-by-ValueId, FUNCTION-LOCAL column per function,
// answering "what register file does this op's result land in?" — see the file header. Takes the Std
// module (the source of truth for ops/blocks/functions) and the Target module (whose operand-derived
// `scanFunctionValueCount` sizes the column). EXHAUSTIVE over `StdOp` with no bare `default`, so a new
// op that defines a value fails to compile here rather than defaulting a float into a GPR.
export function buildValueClassColumns(stdModule StdModule, targetModule TargetModule, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns ValueClassColumns
	var columns = ValueClassColumns.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto stdModule.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		columns.push(buildFunctionValueClasses(stdModule, targetModule: targetModule, funcIndex: funcIndex, funcNames: funcNames))
	end 'eachFunc'
	return columns
end 'buildValueClassColumns'

// Build the callee name → `ArgFloatMask` index, once per module. Each backend builds it in its `create`
// and threads the one instance into the argument-move emission (`lowerCall`/`lowerTryCall`), so a float
// argument's XMM destination and the callee's declared parameter type are the ONE fact, never two that
// could disagree.
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE CHECKED INDEX, NOT THE MODULE (A2l)** — so its key set is provably the one
// `FunctionNameIndex.create` refused a duplicate over, rather than whatever the module happened to hold
// when someone called it. See that file's header for why the guarantee is carried by the argument type.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE OF THIS PAIR LEFT: the RESULT-class map is gone.** Its value was
// `returnTypeResultClass(func.returnType)` — a match over a field the index already reaches in two O(1)
// steps — so it was a second copy of a fact rather than a memo of a computation, and `calleeResultClass`
// now reads it through the index. This one stays because `floatParamMaskOf` FOLDS a parameter list: it is
// a real computation, asked twice per direct call op, and recomputing it per lookup would trade a stated
// guarantee for a measured cost.
export function buildCalleeParamClasses(funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns CalleeParamClassMap
	var calleeParamClasses = CalleeParamClassMap.create()
	for func in funcNames.functions 'eachFunc'
		calleeParamClasses.upsert(func.name, value: floatParamMaskOf(func.paramTypes))
	end 'eachFunc'
	return calleeParamClasses
end 'buildCalleeParamClasses'

// The `ArgFloatMask` for one parameter list: bit k set iff parameter k is an f64. EXPORTED because each
// backend folds its OWN function's parameter types through it once per function and hands that single mask
// to the placement rule for every `param` op — the callee-side mirror of `calleeArgFloatMask`, and the
// reason the callee no longer re-walks its parameter types once per parameter.
export function floatParamMaskOf(paramTypes StdTypeArray) returns ArgFloatMask
	var mask = 0 as ArgFloatMask
	for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("floatParamMaskOf: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
		if stdTypeIsFloat(paramType) 'float'
			mask = mask or (1 shl i)
		end 'float'
	end 'eachParam'
	return mask
end 'floatParamMaskOf'

// The per-ARGUMENT float mask for a DIRECT call to `callee` — ONE lookup of the map whose VALUE ALREADY IS
// THAT MASK. It is the name-keyed twin of the `ArgFloatMask` an indirect `callIndirect` carries ON THE OP:
// producing the direct call's mask here lets the DIRECT and INDIRECT argument-move loops share ONE
// slotting+emit path (`emitArgMovesByFloatMask` in each backend), so the two differ ONLY in where the mask
// comes from — a callee NAME here, the op there — and never in how an argument is slotted onto its file or
// emitted.
//
// ⚠ IT USED TO REBUILD THE MASK BIT BY BIT: `argCount` map lookups, each pulling one bit out through a
// `RegClass` and ORing it back into the mask it had just read. That was the same fact derived twice — the
// stored value IS the answer `floatParamMaskOf` computed — and each backend asks for it TWICE per direct
// call op (once to size the outgoing region, once to emit the moves), so the reconstruction ran 2·argCount
// times per call. A rebuild that ever disagreed with the stored mask would route a float through an integer
// register with no compile error, which is the whole failure mode this file exists to make unspellable.
//
// The mask is returned WHOLE rather than truncated to `argCount` bits, and that is deliberate twice over:
// every reader (`argMaskBit` / `abiFileSlotIndex` / `abiStackSlotsBefore`) is only ever asked about an index
// below `argCount`, so the high bits are unreadable; and the truncation itself — `(1 shl argCount) - 1` —
// would overflow the machine word at the perfectly legal `argCount` of `MaxAbiArgs`.
export function calleeArgFloatMask(calleeParamClasses CalleeParamClassMap, callee ByteArray, argCount ParamCount, registerCapacity RegisterArgCapacity) returns ArgFloatMask
	let mask = try calleeParamClasses.get(callee) otherwise 'runtimeChunk'
		return runtimeChunkArgFloatMask(callee, argCount: argCount, registerCapacity: registerCapacity)
	end 'runtimeChunk'

	return mask
end 'calleeArgFloatMask'

// A callee with NO Std function in the merged module must be a `__`-prefixed HAND-ASSEMBLED runtime chunk —
// the mrt_* panic runtime, or the GT `__gt_context_switch` that a builder-built `__gt_await` calls across
// the Std→chunk boundary. Anything else is a genuine resolution bug and panics here: this narrows the old
// unconditional panic rather than removing it.
//
// ⭐ **EVERY CHUNK USES THE GPR-ONLY INTERNAL ABI** — i64 arguments, R8 result, by construction (see the
// X64Runtime / X64GtRuntime headers). That ONE fact is what both fallbacks past this door return, and it is
// stated here because they cannot share a return type: `calleeResultClass` needs the result's register FILE
// (`gpr`), `runtimeChunkArgFloatMask` the per-argument MASK (all-integer, so zero).
function requireRuntimeChunkCallee(callee ByteArray, context String)
	if not isCompilerInternalCallee(callee) 'notRuntimeChunk'
		panic("{context}: no Std function is named '{String.from(callee)}' — every non-runtime callee reaching the lowering is one LowerMaxonToStd resolved against the merged module")
	end 'notRuntimeChunk'
end 'requireRuntimeChunkCallee'

// The argument mask for a runtime chunk: all-integer, per the GPR-only chunk ABI above.
//
// ⛔ **AND A CHUNK MUST FIT THE REGISTER FILE — the ceiling stack arguments did NOT raise.** A chunk is
// hand-written assembly that reads its arguments out of the argument registers and nowhere else; it has no
// prologue that could reach into a caller's outgoing stack region. Until stack arguments landed, an
// overflowing argument to one was caught for free: `argRegisterAt` / `arm64ArgRegisterAt` panicked past the
// end of the file. Routing the overflow to a stack slot instead turned that loud break into a store nothing
// ever loads — a silent wrong answer. So the limit a chunk genuinely has is stated HERE, at the one door a
// call site learns its callee is a chunk at all. (It is `MaxAsyncArgs` again: a mechanism that never joined
// the calling convention keeps the older, lower ceiling, and says so rather than being assumed.)
function runtimeChunkArgFloatMask(callee ByteArray, argCount ParamCount, registerCapacity RegisterArgCapacity) returns ArgFloatMask
	requireRuntimeChunkCallee(callee, context: "calleeArgFloatMask")

	if argCount > registerCapacity 'pastRegisterFile'
		panic("calleeArgFloatMask: the hand-assembled runtime chunk '{String.from(callee)}' is called with {argCount} arguments, but this target passes only {registerCapacity} per register file — a chunk reads its arguments out of the argument registers and has no stack-argument path, so the overflow would be stored where nothing loads it")
	end 'pastRegisterFile'

	return 0
end 'runtimeChunkArgFloatMask'

// ==========================================================================
// ARGUMENT PLACEMENT — the whole cross-target calling-convention contract
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE RULE, ONE SPELLING, BOTH ENDS OF EVERY CALL.** The callee's `param` lowering and the caller's
// argument moves must place argument `k` identically, or a value is written where nothing reads it — a
// silent wrong answer, never a compile error. So the rule is stated here exactly once and both ends
// consult it.
//
// ⚠ IT USED TO BE STATED TWICE: a recomputing `computeParamSlotIndex` on the callee side, and a
// running-counter twin of it inside each backend's argument loop, held together by a comment that said
// "the running twin of `computeParamSlotIndex`". That was survivable while every argument fit a register,
// because two monotone counters over the same list cannot disagree. It stops being survivable the moment
// an argument can OVERFLOW, because "which slot" then means two different things — an index within one
// register FILE, and an index into the single merged outgoing STACK area — and a hand-written counter that
// gets either one wrong is exactly the class of bug the corpus already pins twice (see
// `specs-shv2/x64-stack-arg-disp32.md`).
//
// Every answer comes from the same two inputs: the per-argument `ArgFloatMask` (which BOTH ends already
// hold — see its header) and the target's per-file register capacity. So neither side walks Std types here,
// and neither can be holding a different one.

// How many arguments of ONE register file a target passes in registers. TARGET-SPECIFIC — x64's custom ABI
// has six GPR and six XMM slots, AAPCS64 has eight X and eight D — so it travels as an argument rather than
// living here as a constant: this file owns the RULE and never any one ISA's register count. Each backend
// reads its own off the argument-register array itself (`x64RegisterArgCapacity` /
// `arm64RegisterArgCapacity`), so adding a register to a file moves the capacity by construction.
//
// ⚠ ONE capacity, not one per file, and that is a real precondition rather than a simplification: the rule
// below asks it of whichever file the argument belongs to. Both backends assert their two files are the
// same depth where they compute it — a shallower float file would silently overflow floats into stack slots
// the callee expects integers in.
// The upper bound is the size of an ISA REGISTER FILE, not the argument ceiling: no architecture shv2
// targets has more than 32 registers in one file (x86-64 has 16 GPRs and 16 XMMs, AArch64 32 and 32), so a
// capacity past that is a mis-derived count rather than a real ABI. It is deliberately NOT `MaxAbiArgs` —
// the two answer different questions and their agreeing at some number would be a coincidence — and a
// range bound must be a literal in any case (`MaxAbiArgs` cannot appear here).
export typealias RegisterArgCapacity = int(1 to 32)

// The index of argument `index` WITHIN ITS OWN REGISTER FILE — the count of preceding arguments that share
// its file. The integer and float files are counted INDEPENDENTLY (v1's internal convention), so a float at
// source position 1 following an integer is float-slot 0, not slot 1.
export function abiFileSlotIndex(mask ArgFloatMask, index ParamCount) returns ParamCount
	let isFloat = argMaskBit(mask, argIndex: index)
	var slotIndex = 0 as ParamCount
	for i in 0 upto index 'countPreceding'
		if argMaskBit(mask, argIndex: i) == isFloat 'sameFile'
			slotIndex = slotIndex + 1
		end 'sameFile'
	end 'countPreceding'
	return slotIndex
end 'abiFileSlotIndex'

// Whether argument `index` travels on the STACK: the arguments before it have spoken for every register in
// its file. THE predicate — no site re-derives "past the end of the register file", so the callee's decision
// to READ from memory and the caller's decision to WRITE to memory are the same decision.
export function abiArgIsOnStack(mask ArgFloatMask, index ParamCount, capacity RegisterArgCapacity) returns bool
	return abiFileSlotIndex(mask, index: index) >= capacity
end 'abiArgIsOnStack'

// How many of the arguments BEFORE `index` travel on the stack. Two readers, one walk:
//   • given an argument's own index, it IS that argument's outgoing stack slot;
//   • given the whole argument count, it is how many stack slots the call needs.
//
// ⭐ THE STACK AREA IS ONE MERGED RUN OF 8-BYTE SLOTS SHARED BY BOTH FILES, which is where it stops
// mirroring the registers. A float that overflows the last XMM slot takes the next stack slot after an
// integer that overflowed the last GPR slot: there is one outgoing region and one stride, so the merged
// count — not either file's own — is what indexes it. (v1's `computeOverflowArgIndex`, restated over the
// mask.)
export function abiStackSlotsBefore(mask ArgFloatMask, index ParamCount, capacity RegisterArgCapacity) returns ParamCount
	var fileSlots = 0 as ParamCount
	var floatFileSlots = 0 as ParamCount
	var stackSlots = 0 as ParamCount
	for i in 0 upto index 'eachPreceding'
		if argMaskBit(mask, argIndex: i) 'floatArg'
			if floatFileSlots >= capacity 'floatOverflows'
				stackSlots = stackSlots + 1
			end 'floatOverflows'
			floatFileSlots = floatFileSlots + 1
		end 'floatArg' else 'intArg'
			if fileSlots >= capacity 'intOverflows'
				stackSlots = stackSlots + 1
			end 'intOverflows'
			fileSlots = fileSlots + 1
		end 'intArg'
	end 'eachPreceding'
	return stackSlots
end 'abiStackSlotsBefore'

// One function's column. Pre-sized to `scanFunctionValueCount` (a LOWER bound — operand-used values)
// and EXTENDED by `setValueClass` for the walk-defined values beyond it. `gpr` is the FILL for ids no
// op defines (nothing is ever coloured for them).
function buildFunctionValueClasses(stdModule StdModule, targetModule TargetModule, funcIndex FuncIndex, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns ValueClassColumn
	let srcFunc = stdModule.getFunc(funcIndex)
	var column = ValueClassColumn.create()
	column.refill(scanFunctionValueCount(targetModule, func: srcFunc), value: RegClass.gpr)

	for blockRef in srcFunc.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		// ⚠ SKIP THE TARGET-ONLY BLOCKS. By the time this runs, `srcFunc.blockRefs` may also hold blocks
		// a backend minted during lowering (x64's float-equality `ordered` split) that exist ONLY in
		// `targetModule.blocks` — looking one up in `stdModule.blocks` is out of range. Such a block
		// holds only branches and defines no value, so skipping it is CORRECT, not merely safe.
		// `prepareSkeleton` made the two block arrays 1:1, so everything appended after is Target-only,
		// which is exactly what this bound means. (arm64 mints no such block, so the guard never fires.)
		if blockRef >= stdModule.blocks.count() 'targetOnlyBlock'
			continue
		end 'targetOnlyBlock'

		recordBlockArgClasses(stdModule, blockRef: blockRef, column: column)
		recordBlockOpClasses(stdModule, blockRef: blockRef, column: column, funcNames: funcNames)
	end 'eachBlock'

	return column
end 'buildFunctionValueClasses'

// A PHI declares its own type, so its class needs no inference from its incoming values — which is
// what lets this stay a single forward walk with no propagation.
function recordBlockArgClasses(stdModule StdModule, blockRef BlockRef, column ValueClassColumn)
	let block = try stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("recordBlockArgClasses: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
	for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachArg'
		setValueClass(column, valueId: arg.valueId, class: regClassForStdType(arg.argType))
	end 'eachArg'
end 'recordBlockArgClasses'

function recordBlockOpClasses(stdModule StdModule, blockRef BlockRef, column ValueClassColumn, funcNames FunctionNameIndex)
	let block = try stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("recordBlockOpClasses: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
	for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
		let op = try stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("recordBlockOpClasses: opIdx {opIdx} not in stdModule.ops")
		recordOpResultClass(op, column: column, funcNames: funcNames)
	end 'eachOp'

	if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
		// A terminator defines nothing today, but it goes through the same exhaustive match rather than
		// being skipped — so an op that both terminates and defines is a compile error here rather than
		// a value with no class.
		let term = try stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("recordBlockOpClasses: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
		recordOpResultClass(term, column: column, funcNames: funcNames)
	end 'hasTerm'
end 'recordBlockOpClasses'

function recordOpResultClass(op StdOp, column ValueClassColumn, funcNames FunctionNameIndex)
	match op 'result'
		// The op's own type IS the result's.
		const(resultId, _, valueType) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: regClassForStdType(valueType))
		// add/sub/mul/div all produce what they consume.
		binOp(resultId, _, _, _, operandType) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: regClassForStdType(operandType))
		unaryOp(resultId, _, opcode, operandType) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: unaryResultClass(opcode, operandType: operandType))
		// ⚠ AN i1, NEVER THE OPERAND TYPE. A float compare's answer is a bool in a GPR.
		cmp(resultId, _, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// The immediate forms are INTEGER by construction — no float op takes an immediate, so
		// `foldConstOperands` cannot produce a float one. Their `operandType` is read for the
		// WIDTH (X5) and never for the class, which is why it is `_` here and a GPR outright:
		// answering `regClassForStdType(operandType)` would be the same answer by a route that
		// stops being obviously true the day a float immediate exists.
		binOpImm(resultId, _, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		cmpImm(resultId, _, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// The TRAPPING integer division; float division is `binOp(div)` above.
		div(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		mod(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		param(resultId, _, paramType) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: regClassForStdType(paramType))
		call(resultId, callee, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: calleeResultClass(funcNames, callee: callee))
		// A throwing call classes its primary result exactly as `call` does (the callee's return type);
		// its error-flag second result is an i64, which the column's `gpr` default already covers (P1.4b).
		tryCall(resultId, _, callee, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: calleeResultClass(funcNames, callee: callee))
		// An ADDRESS is an integer, whatever the global holds.
		globalAddr(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		loadIndirect(resultId, _, _, valueType) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: regClassForStdType(valueType))
		// A page base is an address, so it is an integer — never float-classed.
		osAllocPages(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// An `.rdata` address is an integer, never float-classed.
		rdataAddr(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// A `.text` function address is an integer, exactly like `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr` — never
		// float-classed (P1.5-A1).
		funcAddr(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// A call THROUGH a function value classes its result by the `resultType` the op carries (P1.5 #78): a
		// float-returning function value is XMM-classed and captured from xmm0/d0, everything else GPR from
		// R8/x0. There is no callee NAME to look a return type up against as `call`/`tryCall` do, so the front
		// end's own type for the result value rides on the op (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerIndirectCall`).
		callIndirect(resultId, _, resultType, _, _) then classifyIndirectResult(column, resultId: resultId, resultType: resultType)
		// The ops that define NOTHING. Named individually rather than collapsed into a range: `StdOp`'s
		// declaration order interleaves value-defining ops (`param`, `call`) between the non-defining
		// ones, so a tempting range would span them. Naming each keeps the band-append rule enforcing it.
		condBranch to branch then break 'result'
		ret then break 'result'
		retVoid then break 'result'
		storeIndirect then break 'result'
		// `errorReturn` (a terminator) and `osExit` define no value (P1.4b).
		errorReturn to osExit then break 'result'
		// The subprocess wait result is a GPR i64 (the `WaitForSingleObject` return code `proc_check` reads).
		osWaitHandle(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// `osPanic` defines no value (P1.9) — it aborts. `osReadClock`/`osSleepMs` (P1.5 mid-body-yield)
		// define no value either: the clock read writes its results to caller-provided out-param buffers, and
		// the OS sleep returns nothing. The four result-less P1.5 subprocess ops (`osProcessSpawn`,
		// `osProcessExitCode`, `osWaitHandles`, `osCloseHandle`) join the range — they consume their `BOOL` via
		// memory or ignore it, so none defines a value (`osWaitHandle`, which does, is named just above).
		osPanic to osCloseHandle then break 'result'
		// `osFreePages` (P1.5-B1a′) defines no value — it releases a page region and returns nothing (its
		// `VirtualFree` BOOL is ignored, like `osCloseHandle`'s). It sits past `osWaitHandle` in the union, so
		// it takes its own break arm rather than joining the range above.
		osFreePages then break 'result'
		// The jump table defines no value — a terminator has no result class to record.
		switch then break 'result'
		// A witness call classes its result by the `resultType` the op carries — the same register-file
		// question as `callIndirect` (there is no callee NAME to look a return type up against).
		witnessCall(resultId, _, _, resultType, _, _) then classifyIndirectResult(column, resultId: resultId, resultType: resultType)
		// A THROWING witness call classes its primary result exactly as `witnessCall` does (off the `resultType`
		// the op carries); its error-flag second result is an i64, which the column's `gpr` default already
		// covers — the same split `tryCall`'s two results take.
		witnessTryCall(resultId, _, _, _, resultType, _, _) then classifyIndirectResult(column, resultId: resultId, resultType: resultType)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the five value-defining IOCP ops define GPR i64 results (kernel handles, the
		// ReadFile BOOL, the GetLastError DWORD — all integers); the rest define nothing.
		osPipeCreate then break 'result'
		osIocpCreate(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osEventCreate(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osThreadCreate(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osLockInit to osLockLeave then break 'result'
		osReadOverlapped(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osLastError(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osProcessSpawnInherit then break 'result'
		osCancelIo then break 'result'
		osGetPid(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: osWriteFile defines the WriteFile BOOL (an integer, GPR); osPipeCreateOutbound defines nothing.
		osPipeCreateOutbound then break 'result'
		osWriteFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// The wall-clock read defines no value — `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime` writes its answer to the
		// caller's out-param word, exactly as `osReadClock` does with its two.
		osReadWallClock then break 'result'
		// R4.1: the file OPEN defines the `CreateFileA` HANDLE — an integer, so a GPR.
		osFileOpenRead(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// The close defines no value, so there is no result class to record.
		osFileClose then break 'result'
		// R4.2: the five ops whose Win32 entry point answers in RAX define an integer (a BOOL verdict or a
		// search HANDLE), so a GPR; the find-close defines nothing, exactly as the file close does.
		osReadFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileOpenWrite(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileSize(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileDelete(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileRename(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileFindFirst(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileFindClose then break 'result'
		// R4.3: all four directory primitives answer in RAX with an integer — a Win32 BOOL, a character
		// count or a `dwFileAttributes` mask — so all four define a GPR result.
		osFileFindNext(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osDirCreate(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osDirCurrentPath(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osFileAttributes(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// A3n: a rendered panic defines no value, exactly as `osPanic` defines none.
		osPanicValue then break 'result'
		osCommandLine(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// W5: both answer in RAX with an integer — a kernel HANDLE and a character count — so both define a
		// GPR result.
		osStdHandle(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osExecutablePath(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// W35: both stream writes define the OS's byte COUNT — an integer, so a GPR. `osWriteStdout` used to
		// sit in the defines-nothing group above; it moved here when it acquired that result.
		osWriteStdout(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osWriteStderr(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// A promoted record's address is a POINTER, so its result is a GPR — `globalAddr`'s class exactly.
		stackRecordAddr(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// O3: a HANDLE, a mapped ADDRESS and a tick COUNT are all machine words.
		osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osMapNamedSegment(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osTickCountMs(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// The attached subprocess's two ANSWERING host primitives: a peeked byte count and an environment
		// variable's character count are both machine words.
		osPipePeek(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osEnvRead(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		// Its other two answer nothing at all — the `SetHandleInformation`/`TerminateProcess` BOOL is
		// discarded, exactly as `osCloseHandle`'s is — so there is no result class to record. They take a
		// `break` arm like every other value-less op in this match; this match is EXHAUSTIVE, so "absent" is
		// not a state an op can be in. (It said they were absent, two lines above their own arms.)
		osHandleSetInherit then break 'result'
		osProcessTerminate then break 'result'
		// The socket family's six ANSWERING primitives: a Winsock startup code, a getaddrinfo status, a raw
		// SOCKET, a connect status and two byte counts are all machine words. The other two discard their
		// answer and take a `break` arm like every other value-less op in this exhaustive match.
		osSocketStartup(resultId, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osSocketResolve(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osSocketFreeAddrInfo then break 'result'
		osSocketCreate(resultId) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osSocketConnect(resultId, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osSocketSend(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osSocketRecv(resultId, _, _, _) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: RegClass.gpr)
		osSocketClose then break 'result'
	end 'result'
end 'recordOpResultClass'

// Class an indirect call's result. A VALUE callee's result is classed by its return type (a float-returning
// function value is XMM/SIMD-classed and captured from xmm0/d0, everything else GPR from R8/x0). A VOID
// callee (a raw element destructor) DEFINES no value — the backends emit no capture move for it (see
// `lowerCallIndirect`), so `resultId` never enters coloring and takes no class. Unlike a void DIRECT `call`,
// whose return register IS captured into a garbage-i64 result, a void indirect call has genuinely nothing to
// capture, so classing its dead slot would reserve a register no instruction ever writes or reads.
function classifyIndirectResult(column ValueClassColumn, resultId ValueId, resultType StdReturnType)
	match resultType 'r'
		void then break 'r'
		value(inner) then setValueClass(column, valueId: resultId, class: regClassForStdType(inner))
	end 'r'
end 'classifyIndirectResult'

// The class of a CALL's result — the callee's return type, looked up by name. A VOID call still
// DEFINES a value (`lowerCall` moves the return register into it unconditionally), so a miss on `void`
// is `gpr` and not an error: the value is a garbage integer nobody may read. Exported so `lowerCall`
// picks the SAME capture register (XMM0/d0 for a float result, else R8/x0) the column classes the
// result under — one door, so the capture and the class cannot disagree.
//
// ⭐ **READ THROUGH THE CHECKED INDEX, NOT OUT OF A MAP THAT COPIED IT (A2l).** The answer is a match over
// the callee's own `returnType` field, so the index reaches it in the same one hash probe the deleted map
// cost plus an array read — and the index is the only structure in the compile whose key set is PROVEN
// duplicate-free. A callee with no Std function is the runtime-chunk case the miss arm below owns, exactly
// as before: `funcNamed` misses on the same names the map missed on.
export function calleeResultClass(funcNames FunctionNameIndex, callee ByteArray) returns RegClass
	let func = try funcNames.funcNamed(callee) otherwise 'runtimeChunk'
		requireRuntimeChunkCallee(callee, context: "calleeResultClass")
		return RegClass.gpr
	end 'runtimeChunk'

	return returnTypeResultClass(func.returnType)
end 'calleeResultClass'
